{
    "test_name": "MOB.820_Search_Filter_Then_Search",
    "details": {
        "name": "MOB.820_Search_Filter_Then_Search",
        "type": "browser",
        "status": "paused",
        "message": "`MOB.820` **Appendix D Q9** \u2014 does submitting the search box silently discard the\nactive structured filters?\n- READ-ONLY. Filters and searches only.\n- `AssetLookup/index.tsx` sends the filter conditions twice and the two disagree:\n  `useQuery` (line 62) uses `props.query || query || []` \u2014 **with** filters \u2014 while the\n  search form's `refetch` (line 142) uses `props.query ?? []`, which is `[]` on the\n  standalone page. The pill would still show the filter as active while the results\n  ignored it: the UI says filtered, the data is not.\n- \u2705 **ANSWERED 2026-08-12: the filter IS discarded.** `bugs_found.md` \u00a720. This is now a\n  **characterization test** \u2014 it asserts the *buggy* behaviour so the suite stays green\n  and the defect stays pinned. **It will FAIL when the app is fixed**, which is the\n  point: flip the two assertions back and delete \u00a720 rather than repairing it.\n- The racing `setSearchText` re-fire does **not** rescue it \u2014 the filter-less `refetch`\n  response wins in practice. Reasoning from the two code paths alone would have guessed\n  wrong, in the optimistic direction.\n- The filter value is `ZZZZ-NO-SUCH-ASSET`, not the asset name, so the filter pill cannot echo\n  `Pump 0102` onto the page and satisfy its own assertion (trap 5b \u2014 the exact mistake\n  that produced a wrong answer for Q8).",
        "tags": [
            "Mobile",
            "env:dev",
            "Search",
            "read-only"
        ],
        "locations": [
            "gcp:us-west2"
        ],
